  "content": {
    "id": "p98-q27-question",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "27",
    "prompt": "The area of a square $A$ varies directly with the square of its perimeter $P$. Without finding the side length:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p98-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If the perimeter is tripled, by what factor does the area increase?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Factor $9$.",
          "worked": "Since $A=kP^2$, let $A_1$ be the area when the perimeter is $3P$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{A_1}{A}&=\\frac{k(3P)^2}{kP^2}\\\\\\frac{A_1}{A}&=9\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe area increases by a factor of $9$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p98-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What is the constant of proportionality?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=\\frac{1}{16}$.",
          "worked": "For a square of side length $s$, $A=s^2$ and $P=4s$. Since $A=kP^2$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=\\frac{s^2}{(4s)^2}\\\\k&=\\frac{s^2}{16s^2}\\\\k&=\\frac{1}{16}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
